  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Data Sufficiency for Pipes and Cisterns",
    "description": "Judge whether the given statements contain enough independent rate information to answer the question.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach sufficiency as a rate-information question, not a calculation race. For each statement, ask what rate or relationship it determines and whether that is enough for the requested quantity. Cover both-needed, either-alone, inadequate, redundant-capacity, efficiency-ratio, leakage, and three-statement cases. Small calculations are used only to prove sufficiency.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 16, Pipes and Cisterns. Approved scope: Teach data sufficiency using the chapter's two-statement and three-statement patterns. Cover when both fill and empty times are needed, when either of two different descriptions independently determines a rate, when a tank capacity is irrelevant because only fractional rates matter, and when one of several statements is redundant. Include questions involving efficiency ratios, together-time, leakage, and litre-per-minute taps. The goal is to judge sufficiency without doing unnecessary calculation."
  },
